//! core/tasks — running a piece of a program more than once at a time.
//!
//! Two shapes. `parallel` runs a list of work and waits for all of it. `scope`
//! and `spawn` start work that runs *beside* the code that started it: a scope
//! returns when its body and every task spawned into it have finished.
//!
//! ## `parallel`
//!
//! `parallel(ctx, items, f)` runs `f` over every item and answers the results
//! **in the items' order**, whatever order the work finished in, and hands each
//! call the item's own index. Fixing the order buys a program whose answer is
//! the same on every backend and on every run.
//!
//! `f` receives the caller's whole context as its first parameter, so a task can
//! do anything its caller could — read a file, make a request, sleep — and
//! nothing its caller could not. It arrives as a *parameter* rather than by
//! capture because a lambda may not capture an effect-carrying value (SPEC
//! 10.6).
//!
//! Every task has finished before `parallel` returns, so a task cannot outlive
//! the context that granted it. That keeps "a program that never names
//! `host.fs` cannot read a file" true of the program's lifetime and not only of
//! its call graph. `scope`, below, buys background work without giving that up:
//! the waiting moves from the call to the scope.
//!
//! How much of it actually runs at once is a property of the platform, not of
//! the signature:
//!
//! | Backend | Today |
//! |---|---|
//! | JavaScript | genuinely concurrent — it starts the tasks together and awaits them together, so two that wait overlap. Two that *compute* do not: the engine has one thread |
//! | native, `--release` | genuinely **parallel** — each task runs on a thread of its own, on a processor of its own, so two that wait overlap and two that compute overlap |
//! | native, `buri run` | sequential, in index order, on the calling thread |
//!
//! All three answer the same list, and that is the whole of what this module
//! promises.
//!
//! ```buri
//! from "core/io" import * as io;
//! from "core/list" import * as list;
//! from "core/str" import * as str;
//! from "core/tasks" import * as tasks;
//! from "node" import { NodeHost };
//! from "platform/effect" import { Allocator, Stdout, Tasks };
//!
//! export fn main(host: NodeHost): Result<(), Str> {
//!     let ctx = context {
//!         Allocator: host.alloc,
//!         Stdout: host.stdout,
//!         Tasks: host.tasks,
//!     };
//!     let labelled = tasks.parallel(ctx, ["a", "b", "c"], fn(c, i, name) => {
//!         str.format(c, "${i}:${name}")
//!     });
//!     let _ = io.println(ctx, labelled.join(ctx, " ")).ignore();
//!     .Ok(())
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! prints `0:a 1:b 2:c` — the items' order, whichever task finished first.
//!
//! ## `scope` and `spawn`
//!
//! `parallel` is for work you have a list of. Background work is not that
//! shape: a socket loop, a retry, a timer that fires in five minutes. Those
//! start once and run beside everything else.
//!
//! `scope(ctx, body)` opens a place for that work and closes it. `spawn(ctx,
//! scope, f)` puts one task there. The scope returns when the body **and every
//! task spawned into it** have finished, so nothing runs after the call that
//! opened it.
//!
//! ```buri
//! from "core/io" import * as io;
//! from "core/tasks" import * as tasks;
//! from "core/time" import * as time;
//! from "node" import { NodeHost };
//! from "platform/effect" import { Allocator, Clock, Stdout, Tasks };
//!
//! export fn main(host: NodeHost): Result<(), Str> {
//!     let ctx = context {
//!         Allocator: host.alloc,
//!         Clock: host.clock,
//!         Stdout: host.stdout,
//!         Tasks: host.tasks,
//!     };
//!     let _ = tasks.scope(ctx, fn(c, here) => {
//!         let _ = tasks.spawn(c, here, fn(c2) => {
//!             let _ = time.sleep(c2, time.milliseconds(5));
//!             let _ = io.println(c2, "the timer fired").ignore();
//!             ()
//!         });
//!         io.println(c, "the body is done").ignore()
//!     });
//!     .Ok(())
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! **A task that sleeps is a timer**, for code that holds a scope. Code that
//! holds only a context — a callback, a handler — uses `after`, below.
//!
//! **`Scope` is inert.** It holds no context, so a lambda may capture one (SPEC
//! 10.6) — which is what lets an interface hand a scope to a handler that will
//! spawn into it later.
//!
//! **A library cannot spawn.** It exposes a `run` and the application spawns
//! it, because a scope is the application's to open. `core/net/server`'s `run`
//! is already that shape.
//!
//! **Stopping is cooperative.** A spawned task can't be cancelled. It ends when its own
//! body ends, so a loop stops by finding its socket closed, or by asking an
//! actor whether to carry on. An abort in this language is a write to standard
//! error and an exit (SPEC 6.9), not something a second task can survive.
//!
//! ### When a spawned task actually runs
//!
//! On a native `--release` build a spawned task starts at once, on a thread of
//! its own, while the body keeps running.
//!
//! Everywhere else the body runs first: under `buri run`, on JavaScript, and in
//! a test on every backend, so a `TestTasks` double still decides the order.
//! Then the scope runs what was spawned in rounds through `parallel`, one round
//! for whatever the last one spawned, until nothing is waiting. On JavaScript
//! the tasks of a round overlap. Under `buri run` they run one after another,
//! in the order they were spawned.
//!
//! Under `buri run` a spawned task that never ends starves the ones behind it,
//! because they wait for a round that never finishes. Spawn a socket loop and a
//! timer, and both run on JavaScript and under `--release`, while under `buri
//! run` the timer never starts (`design/native/DECISIONS.md`).
//!
//! A task spawned **after** its scope's body has returned — which on a page is
//! what a handler does — runs on the task that spawned it, because the scope's
//! own drain has already ended. A page is the outer scope and it closes when
//! the page does (`design/native/DECISIONS.md`).
//!
//! ## `after` and `cancel`
//!
//! `after(ctx, duration, run)` runs `run` once `duration` has passed and
//! answers a `Timer` at once; `cancel(ctx, timer)` stops it. Both need only
//! `Tasks`, so a callback that holds nothing but its context can schedule, on
//! every platform.
//!
//! ```buri
//! from "core/io" import * as io;
//! from "core/tasks" import * as tasks;
//! from "core/time" import * as time;
//! from "node" import { NodeHost };
//! from "platform/effect" import { Allocator, Stdout, Tasks };
//!
//! fn tick<C: Stdout + Tasks>(ctx: C, n: Int): () {
//!     let _ = io.println(ctx, "tick ${n}").ignore();
//!     if (n < 3) {
//!         let _ = tasks.after(ctx, time.milliseconds(10), fn(c) => tick(c, n + 1));
//!         ()
//!     } else {
//!         ()
//!     }
//! }
//!
//! export fn main(host: NodeHost): Result<(), Str> {
//!     let ctx = context {
//!         Allocator: host.alloc,
//!         Stdout: host.stdout,
//!         Tasks: host.tasks,
//!     };
//!     let _ = tasks.after(ctx, time.milliseconds(10), fn(c) => tick(c, 1));
//!     .Ok(())
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! prints `tick 1`, `tick 2` and `tick 3` after `main` has returned: a pending
//! timer keeps the program running until it fires or is cancelled. A `main`
//! that answers `.Err`, an abort and `exitWith` end it at once.
//!
//! On JavaScript a timer is a `setTimeout`. A native program fires it on
//! `main`'s thread whenever that thread waits — a sleep, a fetch, a `parallel`
//! being joined — and after `main` returns, never in the middle of code that
//! is computing.

from "core/alloc" import * as alloc;
from "core/time" import { Duration };
from "platform/effect" import { Allocator, Tasks };

/// Runs `step` over every item and answers the results in the items' order.
///
/// The `Int` `step` receives is the item's own index, so a task knows where it is
/// without the closure counting.
///
/// An empty list answers an empty list and runs nothing.
export fn parallel<C: Allocator + Tasks, A, B>(
    ctx: C,
    items: [A],
    step: fn(C, Int, A) => B,
): [B] {
    ctx.parallel(ctx, items, step)
}

// --- Background work ----------------------------------------------------------

/// Where spawned tasks run, and what waits for them.
///
/// Inert data: one handle the runtime issued, and no context. A lambda may
/// capture one (SPEC 10.6), which is what lets a page hand a scope to a
/// handler that spawns into it later. The handle stays this module's own: a
/// scope is the authority to spawn, not the number it is spelled with.
export struct Scope(Int);

/// Opens a scope, runs `body` in it, and answers what `body` answered — once
/// every task spawned into it has finished.
///
/// The body is handed the caller's whole context and the scope, in that order.
/// Nothing spawned here outlives this call: a task cannot outlive the context
/// that granted it.
///
/// A body that spawns nothing costs one round through the runtime to find that
/// nothing is waiting.
export fn scope<C: Allocator + Tasks, A>(ctx: C, body: fn(C, Scope) => A): A {
    let here = Scope(scopeOpen(ctx));
    let answer = match (scopeBeside(ctx, here.0)) {
        true => beside(ctx, here, body),
        false => {
            let run = body;
            run(ctx, here)
        },
    };
    let _ = draining(ctx, here);
    let _ = resting(ctx, here);
    answer
}

/// Starts `step` in `scope`, and answers without waiting for it.
///
/// `step` is handed the caller's whole context, so a task may do anything the code
/// around it could and nothing more: a lambda may not capture an
/// effect-carrying value (SPEC 10.6).
///
/// `core/alloc`'s `copyAcross` deep-copies the task **out of every arena**
/// before it is queued, because the runtime holds it past this call. Without
/// it a task spawned inside an `alloc.scoped` body would reach the runtime in
/// pages the scope is about to unmap.
///
/// A spawn that arrives while the scope's own drain is running is picked up by
/// that drain. One that arrives after it has ended — which on a page is what a
/// handler does — runs here, on the task that spawned it.
export fn spawn<C: Allocator + Tasks>(ctx: C, scope: Scope, step: fn(C) => ()): () {
    let _ = scopePush(ctx, scope.0, alloc.copyAcross([carried(step)]));
    match (scopeEnter(ctx, scope.0)) {
        false => (),
        true => {
            let _ = draining(ctx, scope);
            resting(ctx, scope)
        },
    }
}

// --- Timers -------------------------------------------------------------------

/// A pending run, returned by `after` and stopped by `cancel`.
///
/// Inert data: a number the runtime issued, and no context. A lambda may
/// capture one (SPEC 10.6), which is what lets a callback hold the timer it
/// means to cancel. The authority to cancel travels through `ctx`.
export struct Timer(Int);

/// Runs `run` once `duration` has passed, and answers a `Timer` that names the
/// pending run. Answers at once: nothing waits here.
///
/// `run` is handed a context of its own when it fires, the same one `after`
/// was called with, because a lambda may not capture one (SPEC 10.6).
///
/// It needs no `Scope`, so a callback that holds only a context can schedule:
/// a heartbeat after each frame, a retry after a failed send. A periodic tick
/// is a `run` that calls `after` again.
///
/// The duration is read in whole milliseconds, and a length of zero or less
/// fires as soon as the program next waits. A pending timer keeps the program
/// running after `main` returns, until it has fired or been cancelled.
///
/// `core/alloc`'s `copyAcross` copies `run` out of every arena first, because
/// the runtime keeps it past this call.
export fn after<C: Tasks>(ctx: C, duration: Duration, run: fn(C) => ()): Timer {
    let fire: fn(C, Int) => () = alloc.copyAcross(fn(c, _handle) => run(c));
    Timer(timerStart(ctx, duration.milliseconds(), fire))
}

/// Stops a pending timer. A timer that has already fired, or already been
/// cancelled, is a no-op, so a caller never has to ask which state it is in.
export fn cancel<C: Tasks>(ctx: C, timer: Timer): () {
    timerStop(ctx, timer.0)
}

// --- Beside the body ----------------------------------------------------------

/// Runs `body` and the scope's workers at once, and answers what `body`
/// answered once they have all finished.
///
/// The workers start what the body spawns as soon as it is spawned, each task
/// on a thread of its own.
fn beside<C: Allocator + Tasks, A>(ctx: C, here: Scope, body: fn(C, Scope) => A): A {
    let halves: [Option<A>] = parallel(ctx, [true, false], fn(c, i, isBody) => {
        match (isBody) {
            true => {
                let run = body;
                let answer = run(c, here);
                let _ = scopeRan(c, here.0);
                .Some(answer)
            },
            false => {
                let _ = working(c, here);
                .None
            },
        }
    });
    match (halves.first()) {
        .Some(.Some(answer)) => answer,
        // Unreachable: `parallel` answers in the items' order, so the first
        // answer is the body's. Running it here keeps a toolchain bug from
        // stopping the program.
        _ => {
            let run = body;
            run(ctx, here)
        },
    }
}

/// Runs claimed tasks until the scope is done.
///
/// A worker that claims a task while no other worker is free splits in two
/// first, so there is always one free for the next spawn and a task that
/// never ends holds up nothing spawned after it. A self tail call, so a worker
/// that runs a million tasks uses one frame.
fn working<C: Allocator + Tasks>(ctx: C, scope: Scope): Bool {
    let at = scopeClaim(ctx, scope.0);
    match (at < 0) {
        true => true,
        false => {
            match (scopeSpare(ctx, scope.0)) {
                true => {
                    let _ = ran(ctx, scope, at);
                    working(ctx, scope)
                },
                false => {
                    let _ = parallel(ctx, [at, -1], fn(c, i, claimed) => {
                        match (claimed < 0) {
                            true => working(c, scope),
                            false => {
                                let _ = ran(c, scope, claimed);
                                working(c, scope)
                            },
                        }
                    });
                    true
                },
            }
        },
    }
}

/// The claimed task at `at`, run, and counted as finished.
fn ran<C: Allocator + Tasks>(ctx: C, scope: Scope, at: Int): Bool {
    let _ = running(ctx, scope, at);
    scopeRan(ctx, scope.0)
}

// --- The drain ----------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `draining` is a self tail call, so a scope that runs a million tasks uses one
// frame (`middle/tail_calls.rs`).

/// Every task waiting in `scope`, and everything those tasks spawn, run to the
/// end.
///
/// One **round** at a time: the runtime moves what is waiting into the round
/// and answers that round's indices, `parallel` runs them, and whatever they
/// spawned is the next round. So a task spawned by a task is waited for, and
/// the overlap inside a round is `parallel`'s.
fn draining<C: Allocator + Tasks>(ctx: C, scope: Scope): () {
    let round = scopeRound(ctx, scope.0);
    match (round.isEmpty()) {
        true => (),
        false => {
            let _ = parallel(ctx, round, fn(c, i, at) => running(c, scope, at));
            draining(ctx, scope)
        },
    }
}

/// Gives the drain up, and takes it back where something arrived while it was
/// being given up.
///
/// The one race this module has: a task that spawns just as the drain is
/// ending would otherwise wait for a drain that has already stopped. So
/// `scopeLeave` answers whether anything is waiting, and this takes the drain
/// back when it is.
fn resting<C: Allocator + Tasks>(ctx: C, scope: Scope): () {
    match (scopeLeave(ctx, scope.0)) {
        false => (),
        true => {
            match (scopeEnter(ctx, scope.0)) {
                false => (),
                true => {
                    let _ = draining(ctx, scope);
                    resting(ctx, scope)
                },
            }
        },
    }
}

/// The round's task at `at`, run once.
///
/// `false` where the slot is already empty. Answering a `Bool` rather than `()`
/// keeps `parallel`'s result element above zero bytes.
fn running<C: Allocator + Tasks>(ctx: C, scope: Scope, at: Int): Bool {
    match (taking(ctx, scope, at)) {
        .None => false,
        .Some(held) => {
            match (held.first()) {
                .None => false,
                .Some(wrapped) => {
                    let task = wrapped.value;
                    let _ = task(ctx);
                    true
                },
            }
        },
    }
}

/// The round's task at `at`, **at the type it was spawned at**.
///
/// The entries are type-erased at the key, so the type argument written at the
/// call is the only thing that says what the block holds, and the compiler
/// generates the release from it. A bare `scopeTaskAt` here would leave `T`
/// determined by nothing and release a block that lets go of nothing.
fn taking<C: Tasks>(ctx: C, scope: Scope, at: Int): Option<[Carried<fn(C) => ()>]> {
    scopeTaskAt(ctx, scope.0, at)
}

/// One task on its way through the runtime.
///
/// A `[T]` is two words whatever `T` is (VALUE-MODEL.md §4), so nothing about
/// the element crosses and the runtime needs no stride, no glue and no
/// descriptor to hold a task, queue it and give it back. The `Int` is there to
/// be *some* bytes: a `[T]` whose element occupies none has no block at all,
/// and `first` would answer `.None` at any index.
struct Carried<T> {
    /// Zero, always.
    pad: Int,
    /// The task itself.
    value: T,
}

/// A task, wrapped for the crossing.
fn carried<T>(value: T): Carried<T> {
    Carried { pad: 0, value: value }
}

// --- The runtime's half -------------------------------------------------------
//
// Ten operations for scopes, and two for timers at the end. The two that move a task move a **whole block** — a
// one-element `[Carried<fn(C) => ()>]` — which is what makes them
// implementable against a runtime compiled once that knows no Buri type.
// `middle/monomorphize.rs`'s `GENERIC_INTRINSICS` records the argument, and
// `cli/runtime/lib.rs` §3 the ownership rule they amend.
//
// The runtime never calls one of these tasks. It holds the block, hands it
// back, and `running` above enters it.

/// A fresh scope, and its handle. The caller is its drain until it leaves.
fn scopeOpen<C: Tasks>(ctx: C): Int;

/// Queues one task and answers how many are now waiting. `.None` for a handle
/// naming no scope, and the block is not taken on that path.
fn scopePush<C: Tasks, T>(ctx: C, handle: Int, task: [Carried<T>]): Option<Int>;

/// Moves everything waiting into a round and answers that round's indices.
/// An empty list where nothing is waiting, which is what ends the drain.
fn scopeRound<C: Tasks>(ctx: C, handle: Int): [Int];

/// Takes the round's task at `index`, once. `.None` where it is already taken.
fn scopeTaskAt<C: Tasks, T>(ctx: C, handle: Int, index: Int): Option<[Carried<T>]>;

/// Becomes the scope's drain, where it has none. `false` where somebody else
/// already is, and that somebody will pick up what this caller queued.
fn scopeEnter<C: Tasks>(ctx: C, handle: Int): Bool;

/// Gives the drain up, and answers whether anything is waiting — which is a
/// task that arrived while the drain was ending, and has to be taken back up.
fn scopeLeave<C: Tasks>(ctx: C, handle: Int): Bool;

/// Whether this scope's tasks run beside its body. `true` only in a native
/// `--release` program, and then the body counts as running until `scopeRan`.
fn scopeBeside<C: Tasks>(ctx: C, handle: Int): Bool;

/// The next task for a worker, as an index `scopeTaskAt` takes, or `-1` once
/// nothing is waiting and nothing is running. **Waits** in between.
fn scopeClaim<C: Tasks>(ctx: C, handle: Int): Int;

/// Whether another worker is waiting for the next spawn.
fn scopeSpare<C: Tasks>(ctx: C, handle: Int): Bool;

/// The body, or a claimed task, has finished.
fn scopeRan<C: Tasks>(ctx: C, handle: Int): Bool;

// The timers' two. Unlike a spawned task, a timer's body is the runtime's to
// call: it is kept as a handler is, with the context beside it, and the runtime
// fires it when it is due. The `Int` it is handed is the timer's own handle.

/// Registers `run` to fire once `millis` milliseconds have passed, and answers
/// its handle.
fn timerStart<C: Tasks>(ctx: C, millis: Int, run: fn(C, Int) => ()): Int;

/// Stops the timer `handle` names, where it is still pending.
fn timerStop<C: Tasks>(ctx: C, handle: Int): ();
